{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe mean reversion lag: The model consistently fails to anticipate sharp downward corrections (e.g., drops from ~89 to ~78) following short-term volatility, often predicting a slow drift upward while prices crash. Apply a negative bias adjustment when recent history shows a peak followed by a decline, regardless of flat sentiment.",
    "Adjust for delayed spike recognition: The model significantly underestimates the magnitude and speed of upward spikes (e.g., rising from ~78 to ~95) that occur after a period of low sentiment. When a price recovery begins, increase the forecast slope to match the observed acceleration rather than the model's linear projection.",
    "Reduce forecast overshoot after volatility: The model's linear extrapolation leads to overestimation during the initial phase of a crash and underestimation during the initial phase of a rally. Apply a dampening factor to the forecast slope when the 18-day history variance exceeds the model's implied trend.",
    "Ignore flat sentiment signals: Sentiment history is frequently zero or low even during major price swings, indicating it is a lagging or weak indicator in this dataset. Do not rely on sentiment spikes to trigger forecast adjustments; instead, prioritize momentum changes in the price history itself."
  ]
}
